  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 PM MDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nA backdoor cold front is pushing into the area overnight. Already\nwe are seeing upslope stratus socking in the east slopes of the \nSacramentos, and where the clouds are running aground, some fog.\n\nHREF members are all indicating strong downslope winds on the west\nslopes of the Sacramentos overnight, with gusts in to 40 to 50 mph\nrange. 50 mph gusts are enough for Wind Advisory criteria at EPZ.\nNormally I'd have skipped an advisory for the expected wind gust\nrange, but with all the recent rainfall saturating the soil, and\neast winds having a history of producing more wind damage (they're\nnot uncommon in backdoor front scenarios, but are generally\nlighter and less frequent than the prevailing west winds), I\nfigured we should lean into the potential impacts a bit more and\ngo ahead with an Advisory. We're still stuck using Forecast Zones,\nwhich were mostly created with snow levels in mind. The actual\nhazard area is east of US-54 in the Tularosa Basin, and west of\nthe crest. Most (or all?) of the higher elevation areas along the\nSunspot Highway are on the downslope side, so their zone was\nincluded too. But once you get east of Cloudcroft, the winds will\nbe much lighter, with clouds/fog/drizzle. It'll look a little\nweird on some web site maps, but I did whittle back the Hazard \ngrids to match reality. \n\nOtherwise, expect breezy downslope winds, especially west of the\nHuecos and Franklins tonight, with some gusts around 40-45 mph.\nFar fewer trees (but not zero), and far less rain from Polo down\nhere.\n\nWith a moisture-starved closed low embedded in a trough stalling\nout over the area tomorrow, we'll see clouds blossom after \nsunrise, aided by residual low level moisture and steepening lapse\nrates. Ceilings around 5000-7000 feet. A few spotty showers will\nbe possible, especially over the Sacramentos as the stratus starts\nmixing. \n\nUpper level ridging will bring quiet weather, and a slow warming \ntrend through the weekend, with 80s returning by Sunday. Another\ntrough dropping in from the north mid-week looks interesting with\nits -10C 500mb temps and subtropical jet streak exit region nosing\nin. The sort of pattern that makes me think of convective showers\nwith a bit of small hail. Moisture looks a little lacking, but \nnot bone dry. The models that feed the NBM aren't kicking out \nmuch precip except a slight chance over the Sacs, and we can't \nedit past Day 3. Worth keeping tabs on Wednesday.",
